"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including the preoperative PSA of 6.2 and the pathological T-stage of pT3a with documented extraprostatic extension. Positive findings include negative surgical margins and no seminal vesicle invasion or lymph node metastasis reported from the prostatectomy specimen. The absence of a Gleason pattern or ISUP grade from the biopsy limits prognostic certainty regarding potential discrepancies between biopsy and resection findings. Missing postoperative PSA data prevents assessment of early biochemical recurrence trends, which is critical for refining risk stratification. Future prognosis revision would be significantly aided by obtaining serial postoperative PSA measurements to track trajectory following surgery. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 50.23–75.35 months; its coverage has not been validated."
